The value-tree refusal is a definition: gendrake.ValueRefusalOf returns the reasons, read by ElementRefusalOf and Drake.expression (GitHub #11)

OWNING ISSUE: GitHub #11 (open; stays open). Nothing on the tracker was touched.

WHY. After 101ea48 a definition could read a record's own keys and a value's compact text, which is what the emitter's
`refuseUnrenderable` needed to cross with its messages unchanged. It had two callers: `refuseElement`, where the
definition's name is known, and the public `Drake.expression`, where there is no definition and so no subject for a
Problem. Dev accepted "Accepted, build it" on one new trio returning the reasons as texts, wrapped into Problems where a
subject exists.

WHAT.
- New trio `gendrake.ValueRefusalOf`: factory `par node Dracon`, one element `reasons [Text]`. A text leaf or null has
  none. A single-key node whose key is neither structural (`.`, `()`, the lambda, `(,)`, `[]`, `{}`, `=`) nor a row of
  `draketarget.Fixity.table` is "Drake.expression: unknown operator '<op>' in <compact>"; its operands follow, recursively,
  a single operand that is not a list being one operand. Anything else — a number, a list, an empty or several-key
  record — is "Drake.expression: unrenderable value <compact>". The messages are the emitter's, unchanged. Authored in
  drake with its `uses` record, JSON `DrakeCLI parse`, Scala `GeneratorCLI generate`; listed by GenDrake.
- `gendrake.ElementRefusalOf`: `problems` is the keyword problem, then one Problem per reason of the element's value, then
  the block-value problem, then the recursion.
- Drake.scala: `expression` raises the first of `ValueRefusalOf(value).reasons`; `refuseUnrenderable` and its `structural`
  set are removed; `refuseElement` keeps the formals check alone.
- The §6 generation order was followed (ValueRefusalOf generated and compiled, then ElementRefusalOf and GenDrake); all
  four gendrake trios touched or reading it are stable on the recompiled engine.
- ValueRefusalOfTest (6), the 94th suite: every structural key and every fixity row accepted; an unknown operator named
  with its node; the unrenderable shapes; every depth, every reason in order; the value read once and lazily; the live
  entry point raising the first reason and no value of the corpus refused. ElementRefusalOfTest +1 (the value refusal as
  Problems), its emitter test restated: the hand-judged refusal that follows is now a method's duplicate formals.

DIFFERENCES. (1) As recommended: the refusal is judged over the whole definition before the formals check, and raised as
an IllegalArgumentException where it was a RuntimeException (still one, by subclass). (2) NOT as recommended: I said a
block whose misplaced value is also unrenderable would still be refused for the block first. It is refused for the VALUE
first, the order the hand-written emitter judged them in, and the block problem is not reported for a value that cannot be
spelled — the block check spells the value, and spelling is defined only inside the tree language. (3) Every reason is
reported, where the emitter stopped at the first; the first is the same. No existing test outside the two suites changed.

PROBED IN BUILDING. Both points unprobed at the recommendation held: a single operand that is not a list, and the
structural key list with the lambda's key bound on its own line (the ExpressionOf shape).

NOT BUILT, not accepted: the formals check (`MethodTypeParameters.validate`), shared by the emitter, the parser and the
Scala generator — the one refusal still hand-written on the emit side, and a design question of its own.

